  "query_preview": "/Wednesday through Sunday/...\nIssued 159 PM MDT SUN JUL 19 2026\nSoutherly flow aloft will remain over the area Wednesday, with the \nregion between the upper-level ridge in the south-central U.S. and \nupper-level troughing off the Pacific NW coast. The greatest chances \nfor showers and thunderstorms in the extended period will be on \nWednesday, as an embedded shortwave trough lifts north through the \nGreat Basin. This feature should provide additional lift for \nconvection, with most areas seeing a 20-60% chance of \nshowers/thunderstorms. Precipitable water values will still be \nhovering around the 95th percentile during this time, creating the \nrisk of heavy rain and flash flooding, especially over burn scars \nand steep terrain. Starting Thursday, the flow aloft will turn \nsouthwesterly as an upper trough deepens off the Pacific Northwest \ncoast. This will gradually push deeper moisture to the east with a \ndrying trend from the west through the end of the week. By the \nweekend, only minimal chances for precipitation will persist across \nour eastern zones. Southwest flow will bring hot, dry, and breezy \nconditions late week into the weekend. Smoke will likely continue to \nimpact much of the area through the end of the week, especially \nacross southeast Oregon.",
